OnBytes/Model/TestAttentionHonestData.lean: what the C++ verifier read, drew and reached at the
first attention sub-proof of an honest proof. Data for OnBytes/Model/TestAttentionHonest.lean.

The program does not import this file.

How it was made. The C++ verifier was built from a copy of its source with added lines that only
print. It was run on an honest proof of one request at a small configuration, spelled out in
OnBytes/Model/TestAttentionConfig.lean (exampleScan and the definitions beside it: 64 tokens, eight
layers in two groups of four, four query heads, two heads of keys and values). The proof was made by
a build of the repository's cp_commit and cp_prove for tests, which takes a fixed seed where the
ordinary build draws its own, on synthetic weights. With the added lines the verifier gave the same
answer as without them. Neither the changed copy nor the proof is in the repository, and the
examples under prover/examples may have other numbers. So the data cannot be regenerated from the
repository alone. heard, seenTables and the nine values after them all come from that run. None of
them is the output of a Lean function. shownUnder, factors and filled also follow from heard alone.
The other values can be checked only with the C++ verifier.

What it holds.
 - heard: the calls of the routines through which the sub-proof reads its messages and takes the
   verifier's draws, from its start to the commitment to the tables, as a record
   (OnBytes/Model/TestReplay.lean). With them: the numbers of the C++ verifier's plan, the slot at
   the end, and the element that batches the claims.
 - seenTables: the tables as the smallness test takes them, printed after the last one was built.
 - All other values: the state after the last claim of the sub-proof and of its lookup.
Each vector was printed whole. A script computed its length and the one element that Replay.sumAt
gives. The script is not in the repository. It computes the sum by code of its own, with
arbitrary-precision integers, and does not call the Lean function. A vector is given here as this
pair.
-/
